| 1 | 9 | Staying with a question. *What makes a research question worth weeks of searching, and which sources earn a place in the log?* | 7 | g9-research-question-design; g9-databases-operators; g9-lateral-method; g9-source-log | AASL.I.D.2; AASL.IV.D.1 | Connect, wonder, investigate, reflect | Designing a research question narrow enough to answer and open enough to matter inside a topic the teacher sets, and staying with it over three rounds of searching across weeks 2 to 13, logging how the question shifts (AASL.I.D.2, owned here: "Stay with one research question over weeks, through several rounds of searching"). Searching a database (a free public or open-access one named generically, or an invented one on the paper path) with AND, OR, NOT, an exact phrase and truncation, and filters by date and kind, and comparing it with the open web for the same question. Lateral reading as a fast routine on hard invented cases: stop, investigate the source, look for better coverage, trace the claim to its original, run with the card. An annotated source log with a judgment line and the date each source was last checked, rechecked as the question shifts, sources dropped or added with a reason (AASL.IV.D.1, owned here: "Keep rechecking a collection of sources for quality and usefulness"). Revisited, owned earlier: turning a topic into questions (AASL.I.A.1, g3-research-questions, grade 3) and planning steps and sources (AASL.I.B.2, g4-narrow-and-plan, grade 4); searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4) and choosing sources with a reason (AASL.IV.A.3, g5-choose-sources, grade 5); questioning a source's maker (AASL.IV.B.3, g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5) and judging accuracy and fitness (AASL.VI.A.3, g6-lateral-reading, grade 6). Out: subject terms and following references (unit 3); a self-set topic (grade 10); machine-made text (unit 4). Opening retrievals: day 1 g8-test-assumptions and g8-research-product (write what you expect to find; name last year's product and audience); day 2 g8-multiple-views-gather; day 3 g8-news-lateral; day 4 g8-compare-wikis. Days: show-me (hook: a too-broad and a too-narrow question on the topic; the question designed), show-me (database or open web, operators and filters in the search plan), show-me (the lateral routine card on a polished invented page), practice (three invented claims checked laterally in pairs with the card; an operator string fixed; the source log begun), inquiry (round 2 on the student's own question; log entries; the card withdrawn; the teacher writes feedback on each log before day 6), inquiry (the log rechecked laterally as the question shifts; the shift logged), inquiry (the routine without the card on a hard case from the log; a one-page findings note shared with a partner pair; the self-check). Skill card: "stop, investigate the source, better coverage, trace it." End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year check (day 8) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the revised log scored as its I.D.2 product (Course-level checks). Tools: the research notebook, a closed invented source set of six sources with "other tabs" sheets, an invented database results page, the search plan sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: judging sources of the past, wh1-sources-of-the-past in social studies grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 15), and the short research of ela grade 9 unit 4 (days 67 to 84); stand-alone: "How much do street trees cool a town block in summer?" inside the teacher-set topic "trees and heat in towns" | First because every later unit needs a question held over time and sources judged and kept: grade 8 handed on the expectation, the search plan, lateral reading on news and tracing to references, and grade 9 makes them one long research habit; the four lateral days put most of the band's grade 9 lateral time where the WH1 source checks run in weeks 1 to 10 | Candidate: the 9-10 cells on keeping a list of search terms that worked and on predicting the kinds of sources that will answer; the 9-10 bold on using a database's and an article's own features (abstracts, headings) to find the answering part | Cases even across views; the polished fake page is invented, never a real hoax reproduced. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its source set (C12). Open search only on a teacher-guided inquiry day with the stop rule; the teacher page says what the adult does if an unsafe result appears and points to health's steps if a student tells something worrying (APPROACH.md, Section field 1) |
| 2 | 9 | Fair to every side. *How do we make a case that is fair to every side and honest about whose work it uses?* | 7 | g9-fair-sides; g9-group-shapes-solution; g9-open-licenses | AASL.II.B.3; AASL.III.A.3; AASL.VI.C.1 | Investigate (perspective), construct, express, reflect | Presenting more than one side fairly, each view at its strongest with its best evidence, before the group's own conclusion (AASL.II.B.3, owned here: "Show more than one side fairly in what you make and present"). A research group writes a short decision memo for a neutral school problem in which the choice follows from what the group found, each step citing a member's evidence, and each student says how the findings changed their first idea (AASL.III.A.3, owned here: "Let what the group found shape how you choose to solve a problem"). Reading the license on a work (all rights reserved, open licenses and their conditions, public domain), sharing or remixing only as it allows, crediting as it asks, and writing a polite permission request when it does not allow a use; fair use in depth, the four questions weighed on harder cases (a remix, a parody, a clip inside a video) where reasonable people differ, never ruled lawful (AASL.VI.C.1, owned here: "Share and remix others' work only as its license allows"). Revisited, owned earlier: comparing sources with different views (AASL.II.B.2, g7-sources-disagree, grade 7); honest, permitted use (AASL.VI.B.1, g8-fair-use-basics, grade 8). Out: choosing a license for one's own work (unit 4); one full citation style for every kind of source (unit 6). Opening retrievals: day 10 g8-multiple-views-gather and the lateral routine (unit 1); day 11 g8-fair-use-basics; day 13 g8-diverse-team. Days: show-me (each view at its strongest on an invented case), show-me (reading a license; the four questions on a harder case; the permission request), practice (six works sorted by what their licenses allow; two fair use cases argued both ways; a one-sided paragraph rewritten fairly), inquiry (groups gather each view's best evidence on the decision; card withdrawn), inquiry (the memo drafted, images and quotes used as their licenses allow, a permission request drafted where needed), inquiry (written feedback from the teacher and one other group; the memo revised), inquiry (the memo presented to its audience, a school group the teacher arranges; each student writes how the findings changed their first idea; the self-check). Skill card: "strongest case for each side, best evidence, then our choice; read the license before you use it." End-of-unit evidence: the year-end check (day 17) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the memo's change line scored as its III.A.3 item (Course-level checks). Tools: invented case cards for each view, a license sort set of six works with their labels, two fair use case cards, the memo frame, the permission request frame (Curio Class writes all). Host: comparing interpretations, wh1-sources-and-interpretations in social studies grade 9 unit 3 (days 31 to 46); the group talk of ela grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 22); stand-alone: "Should our school add water refill stations, and where?" | After unit 1 because fair sides need the judged, logged sources unit 1 taught, and the memo needs a question held over weeks; the license work sits where a product first uses other people's images and quotes. Grade 8 fair use basics lead straight into the depth here | Candidate: the 9-10 bold on retrieving and using images, sound and video by their legal and ethical terms (open-license and public domain image sources, named generically); the 9-10 bold on working with others to research a real problem and propose a change | Cases even across views; the school problem is neutral and no partisan stand is asked. Fair use and licenses are general guidance, never legal advice; students weigh the questions and never rule a use lawful (APPROACH.md item 6, `rights` read). Honest use without shaming: a student who used a work wrongly is helped to fix it privately (item 4). Groups are mixed by each student's first answer, never by background |
