5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 8, two a school year (units 1 and 2 in grade 9, 3 and 4 in grade 10, 5 and 6 in grade 11, 7 and 8 in
8:   grade 12), each unit one full inquiry cycle on a host task or its stand-alone question, with its product shared and
9:   the self-check. Each year is whole on its own: its two units, its end-of-unit assessments, a mid-year check and a
10:   year-end check. The order follows the band's growth: grade 9 stays with one question and judges and keeps sources,
11:   then uses them fairly and honestly; grade 10 sets its own topic, brings in missing views and puts sources together;
12:   grade 11 searches beyond articles and shows a reader how the work was done, then publishes; grade 12 builds a
13:   system for after school and ends with the capstone.
14: - **Days.** 72: 18 a year for four years. Each year: 14 days in units and 4 course-level days (a mid-year check and
15:   its respond day, a year-end check and its respond day); band total 56 in units and 16 for checks and respond days.
16:   One session of about 45 minutes, about one session every two weeks across the school year, 18 sessions a year over
17:   32 weeks (APPROACH.md, Pacing and dose: 18 sessions a year of 45 minutes, about 13.5 hours a year and about 54 hours
18:   across the band, as Cory kept them on 2026-11-21). Each session takes one period of a host class (most often ELA or
19:   history), led by the school librarian or the host class's teacher. The 45 minutes hold getting out the research
20:   notebook and the printed set, the session, and collecting work: a lesson plan budgets about 3 minutes at each end,
21:   so about 39 working minutes; a day's opening retrieval takes at most 5 of them. Load: each year carries five to seven
22:   rows; a unit gets about two teaching days a row, more where the row is a long product (the grade 9 research log, the
23:   grade 11 method note and interview, the grade 12 capstone) and fewer where it is a short move added to a product the
24:   unit already makes (labeling one's own work, a database's subject terms). Between sessions the host course carries
25:   the long research work; a class with no host in step runs the unit's stand-alone question on its closed source set
26:   inside the library sessions alone. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (no unit is over 25 days, so
27:   no mid-unit assessment); in units 1 to 7 the course-level check right after the unit is that unit's end-of-unit
28:   assessment, and unit 8 ends in its own (the capstone, day 70). These are course days: course day n of a year falls
29:   on about school day 9n of that year (week 2 for day 1, week 15 for day 8, week 31 for day 17, week 32 for day 18),
30:   and the scope and sequence places them on school days.
31: - **Day kinds.** In library's words (APPROACH.md, The kinds of day; docs/GLOSSARY.md, teaching day): Understanding is
32:   the show-me day (the teacher thinks aloud through a search, a lateral check, a license reading or a citation on an
33:   invented case, holding the unit's skill card); Fluency is the practice day (short, varied tries of one move with
34:   feedback: three claims checked laterally, four sources cited, six works sorted by license); Application is the
35:   inquiry day (students run part of the cycle on the host task or the stand-alone question: question, search, judge,
36:   note, make, share), and drafting and revising a product are inquiry days. Fluency is small: one or two practice days a
37:   unit, plus the five-minute opening retrieval on most days. Every unit's day 1 is a show-me day with the hook and the
38:   compelling question. The skill card is held by the teacher on the first show-me day, by pairs on the first practice
39:   day, and withdrawn on the first inquiry day each unit plan names; every check is taken without it.
40: - **Standard.** AASL National School Library Standards, second edition, learner framework (ids and our own words only,
41:   data/standards/aasl-words.txt; never the competency text, and no claim of alignment approved by anyone). Ownership
42:   rule (STANDARD.md section 3): an id is owned once, in the school year where it is first checked alone. The band owns
43:   18 ids, each in the year whose row checks it alone (grade 9: I.D.2, IV.D.1, II.B.3, III.A.3, VI.C.1; grade 10:
44:   II.A.3, III.C.2, II.C.2, IV.D.2, VI.B.3; grade 11: IV.D.3, II.D.1, VI.C.2, VI.D.2; grade 12: VI.D.1, VI.D.3, I.D.3,
45:   II.D.3; each named with its phrase in its unit), and revisits the ids earlier grades own, each named in its unit with
46:   its owner. Skills: the 24 progression rows of grades 9 to 12, each in one unit. Check set: the Empire State
47:   Information Fluency Continuum, 9 to 12 band file, its 9-10 and 11-12 columns and bold priority skills (read cell by
48:   cell for this frame, later.csv L103; result in the Check set read bullet); the band file has two columns, so grades
49:   9 and 10 are checked against the 9-10 cell and 11 and 12 against the 11-12 cell, and the rows say how each year
50:   differs. Values: in 6 to 12, cases are invented or chosen evenly across views; hoax or hate pages are described or
51:   shown in an invented form, never reproduced (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 1).
52: - **Year differences.** Grade 9 researches inside a topic the teacher sets and runs the lateral routine with its card;
53:   grade 10 sets its own topic and question, and runs the lateral check without the card on machine-made text; grade 11
54:   is the time-pressure and show-your-method year on a short project; grade 12 puts the band to use on a real decision
55:   and on life after school. Grade 9 learns database operators and filters, grade 10 subject terms and following
56:   references, grade 11 reports and data sets, grade 12 saved searches in a personal system.
57: - **Base text.** None in the source store. Every day is Curio Class's own. The partial bases settled for 6 to 12
58:   (STANDARD.md section 3: the CC BY online-reasoning lessons and the open web-literacy book in
59:   docs/reviews/library-open-material-2026-11-16.md) are adapted item by item at the day, only items under an open
60:   license, each license re-read at source when the item is first adapted, credited in the day's credit, with every
61:   named platform replaced by a generic kind. Where they would fit, ours: the lateral reading lessons in units 1 and 4,
62:   news versus opinion, sponsored results and checking photos, video and data in units 4 and 5, the book's four moves
63:   (previous work, go upstream, read laterally, check your own feeling about a claim) as teacher background for units 1
64:   and 5. No source map is written now, since no item is in the source store (For Cory, item 1). Curio Class writes, per
65:   unit, the invented source sets, invented search and database results pages, invented sites and their "other tabs"
66:   sheets, dated captures, the skill cards, the research notebook pages and every check.
67: - **Host tasks.** Every unit runs its cycle on a host task from a core course of the same grade, named by row id for
68:   the teacher to schedule against, and carries a stand-alone host question of its own (an invented question with a
69:   closed source set) so the unit runs when no core task is in step, or at home (APPROACH.md). The scope and sequence
70:   records both. Where a host runs a long research project in the same weeks (ELA grade 10 unit 7, ELA grade 11 unit
71:   4, the grade 12 projects), one question serves both courses where the teachers agree, and library never adds a
72:   second paper.
73: - **The inquiry arc.** Each unit opens with its compelling question and closes with the share step and the
74:   self-check: the student rates their own use of the skill card's steps and names one thing to do differently next
75:   time (APPROACH.md, the form of daily evidence; bar part 11 item 6). All six steps (ask, find, judge, use, share,
76:   reflect; the continuum's connect, wonder, investigate, construct, express, reflect) run in every unit, and the Inquiry
77:   phases column names the phases each unit's rows teach.
78: - **Evidence.** Daily: show-me and practice days end with an exit ticket that asks for the move once on a new case;
79:   inquiry days keep the working notes (question, search terms tried, sources with a judgment line each) in the research
80:   notebook. End-of-unit assessment: a sitting of unseen items, each answered alone in writing in about 35 of the 39
81:   working minutes (a search, a judgment, a license reading or a citation actually done on a new invented case, scored
82:   on a short teacher-judged rubric, plus a few items with one answer checked on the page), and, where a row's check is
83:   a product (the grade 9 research log, the grade 10 seminar record, the grade 11 method note, the grade 12 guide and
84:   capstone), the product scored on the rubric after a draft day, written feedback and a revision counted in the unit's
85:   days. No score from a day a move is first taught. The respond day's groups come from the class record sheet by the
86:   rubric step missed (APPROACH.md, unit field 7). Results go to the student; no per-student record is passed on beyond
87:   what the school keeps for any course.
88: - **Reading level.** Source texts at the grade's level, short (one to two pages a source), with a shorter source set
89:   and read-aloud pages for students who read below it (APPROACH.md, section field 9). A practice day's reading load is
90:   about four short pages; an inquiry day's is the student's own sources.
91: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper: printed invented source sets, printed search and database results pages,
92:   printed "other tabs" sheets that show what other sources say about a site (so lateral moves are made on paper),
93:   dated captures of machine-made summaries and news, the research notebook, and printed license labels. No day needs
94:   a device, an internet connection or a student account, and no student types a name, school, place or photo into a
95:   search or a site (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 3).
96: - **Devices.** None required. On an inquiry day with open search a school may give pairs one shared device, under the
97:   teacher's guidance and the stop rule (close the page, tell the adult, no blame); the student page then says "your
98:   search results" and names no site. No owned or revisited id needs technology: database searching, lateral reading,
99:   publishing (VI.C.2: a one-page brief or an exhibit counts) and labeling one's work are met in full on the paper path.
100:   A tool may give practice on a closed invented set; it never uses the live web, records what a student types, or
101:   writes a student's notes (APPROACH.md, What a tool may and may not do).
102: - **Lateral reading time.** Grades 9 and 10 hold seven sessions, about 315 minutes, whose main move is lateral reading
103:   (unit 1 days 3, 4, 6 and 7; unit 4 days 30, 31 and 32), at least the six 50-minute lessons of the one high school
104:   field study (APPROACH.md, Pacing and dose: about 500 students, from the university's news release, the article not
105:   re-read). No page claims the method raises scores (lens, Day errors). Grades 11 and 12 return to it in units 5 and 8.
106: - **Grade 12 calendar.** The last teaching day is day 70 (week 29), unit 8's capstone and its end-of-unit assessment.
107:   Weeks 31 and 32 hold only the year-end check (day 71) and its respond day (day 72), no new teaching. If seniors leave
108:   before week 31, days 71 and 72 fold: the capstone's score stands as unit 8's end-of-unit evidence, the year-end
109:   check's sampled items are not taken that year, and the respond day's feedback on the capstone goes to each student
110:   in writing. A student 18 or over gets the family note themselves and makes their own leave-out or consent choice
111:   (AGENTS.md), in every year of the band where it applies, the interview units above all.
112: - **Safety read.** No hands-on materials beyond paper and, at most, a shared device; no field work and no trip, so no
113:   laboratory or field guide applies. Interviews outside school (unit 6) follow APPROACH.md "Sensitive ground" item 8:
114:   arranged through the school or a family, in a public or supervised setting, with consent. The screen-safety side is
115:   the stop rule on every open-search day, read by the sensitivity read.
116: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
117:   kind `sensitivity`) on its case set at the unit plan and again at the days; no outside human reader is planned (Cory,
118:   2026-11-14). The license and fair use days (units 2 and 4, and unit 6's honesty review) get the kind `rights` read:
119:   general guidance, never legal advice, no claim that a use is lawful (APPROACH.md item 6); the agent studies first the
120:   US Copyright Office's free fair use index, https://www.copyright.gov/fair-use/ (named here for the agent, not opened
121:   for this frame), and the license summaries of the open license used in each case. Machine-made text (units 4 and 6):
122:   no claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable (item 7). What the agent studies first for every unit: APPROACH.md
123:   "Sensitive ground" items 1 (even views, invented hoaxes), 2 (the stop rule), 3 (privacy), 4 (honest use without
124:   shaming), 5 (whose voices a source set shows, C12) and, for units 3, 6 and 8, item 8 (interviews). When a unit runs on
125:   a host task, the host unit's own sensitivity notes and named sources apply to that content (the host frame's last
126:   column); library's own case sets touch no cultural, Native, hard-history, health or disability content, and the
127:   units that ask about a student's own background or communities (3 and 8) keep every answer optional and private to
128:   the teacher. No organization's name goes on a student, teacher or family page.
129: - **Supplies and cost.** Printing at about 5 cents a page (ours, provisional) for a class of 30, source sets printed
130:   once as a class set and reused by later sections, notebook pages and checks per student: unit 1 about $12, unit 2
131:   about $10, unit 3 about $10, unit 4 about $12, unit 5 about $10, unit 6 about $10, unit 7 about $8, unit 8 about $12;
132:   about $22 a year, about $84 across the band. No paid database, library card or library visit is assumed (Cory,
133:   2026-11-11).
134: - **Check set read (L103, 2026-11-25).** The 9 to 12 band file's 9-10 and 11-12 columns and priority boxes, every
135:   indicator row (S:\CurioClass-sources\library\aasl-2026-11-21\esifc-9-12\esifc-priority-9-12.docx, read as text with
136:   `python -I`, ids and meanings only, nothing copied). Grade 9 and 10 rows against the 9-10 cell, met: key words and
137:   synonyms kept in a search list and refining the question into a framework for the inquiry
138:   (g9-research-question-design, with the search plan); advanced search with operators and filters
139:   (g9-databases-operators); a source's authority judged and evidence chosen across sources (g9-source-log); civic online
140:   reasoning, verifying what is presented as true (g9-lateral-method); the effect of point of view and purpose on a
141:   source (g9-fair-sides, g10-own-lens); an authentic problem researched and a change proposed with others
142:   (g9-group-shapes-solution); a mind map of the big idea and its links, information organized independently, and a
143:   topic refined independently (g10-synthesis-map, grade 10's self-set topic); database features such as abstracts and
144:   subject headings (g10-database-deep); respect and empathy for diverse people, and confidence in questioning views
145:   unlike one's own (g10-others-views-in); misinformation judged by its maker's motives (g10-lateral-machine-text).
146:   Grade 11 and 12 rows against the 11-12 cell, met: publishing for an authentic audience and presenting to it
147:   (g11-publish-for-audience); copyright, fair use, open licenses, credit and permission respected, and one's product
148:   judged and revised from feedback (g11-honesty-review, g12-lateral-capstone); tolerance for different viewpoints
149:   (g11-learn-across-difference); fact-checking before sharing (g11-lateral-news, g12-personal-system); a line of
150:   argument that meets competing interpretations, a claim with evidence and reasoning, and problems with no single best
151:   answer (g12-capstone-decision); sources corroborated and challenged in notes (g12-lateral-capstone); a leadership
152:   role that helps others do their best work (g12-help-others). Differences, each to go into the row's note (Row changes,
153:   listed in the lead's message): truncation, in the 9-10 bold on advanced searching (g9-databases-operators); reading
154:   laterally for context sits in the 11-12 bold, and library teaches it from grade 5 as its own method (g9-lateral-method);
155:   respect for copyright, open licenses and permission is an 11-12 bold, with copyright, fair use and permission plain
156:   in 9-10, so grade 9 checks it at 9-10 depth (g9-open-licenses); citing every source in a set style and embedding
157:   citations is a 9-10 bold (g10-label-own-work); degrees of bias, from a slight slant to propaganda, an 11-12 bold
158:   (g11-lateral-news); contrasting primary and secondary sources and how a results page orders and marks sponsored
159:   results, 11-12 bolds (g11-advanced-search, the second a review of g5-search-revise); using information to look into
160:   next steps after school and setting goals from one's strengths, 11-12 bolds (g12-personal-system). Met without a row
161:   change: plans to test a hypothesis (g8-test-assumptions, revisited at g9-research-question-design); notes in a
162:   chosen form with a reflection column (the research notebook on inquiry days, the grade 10 matrix); records and
163:   reflects on the inquiry (every unit's self-check); curating and sharing resources with peers (g12-help-others and the
164:   shared logs); building a conceptual framework, an 11-12 bold met one year early in g10-synthesis-map, and seeking
165:   opposing views, an 11-12 bold met one year early in g10-others-views-in. Left out: the design thinking standard
166:   (science and computer science, STANDARD.md section 4); response to literature, reading goals and real-world genres
167:   (ELA); visual literacy beyond a chart or photo inside a lateral case (ELA's RI.7 rows and social studies' graphic
168:   display rows); joining social media and its rules of engagement (no student account is assumed); the acceptable use
169:   policy, netiquette and the costs of hacking and fraud (the school's own policy, computer science's security rows
170:   such as computer-science:g10-breach-causes, and health); the SEL items marked in the band file (growth mindset,
171:   relationship skills, responsible decision-making: SEL, as library-excluded.csv places V.D.1, V.D.2 and III.D.2); the
172:   First Amendment and access rights (US Government and Politics). No row is two or more grades from its cell without
173:   a reason in its note once the Row changes land.
