241: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
242: | 1 | 9 | Staying with a question. *What makes a research question worth weeks of searching, and which sources earn a place in the log?* | 7 | g9-research-question-design; g9-databases-operators; g9-lateral-method; g9-source-log | AASL.I.D.2; AASL.IV.D.1 | Connect, wonder, investigate, reflect | Designing a research question narrow enough to answer and open enough to matter inside a topic the teacher sets, and staying with it over three rounds of searching across weeks 2 to 13, logging how the question shifts (AASL.I.D.2, owned here: "Stay with one research question over weeks, through several rounds of searching"). Searching a database (a free public or open-access one named generically, or an invented one on the paper path) with AND, OR, NOT, an exact phrase and truncation, and filters by date and kind, and comparing it with the open web for the same question. Lateral reading as a fast routine on hard invented cases: stop, investigate the source, look for better coverage, trace the claim to its original, run with the card. An annotated source log with a judgment line and the date each source was last checked, rechecked as the question shifts, sources dropped or added with a reason (AASL.IV.D.1, owned here: "Keep rechecking a collection of sources for quality and usefulness"). Revisited, owned earlier: turning a topic into questions (AASL.I.A.1, g3-research-questions, grade 3) and planning steps and sources (AASL.I.B.2, g4-narrow-and-plan, grade 4); searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4) and choosing sources with a reason (AASL.IV.A.3, g5-choose-sources, grade 5); questioning a source's maker (AASL.IV.B.3, g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5) and judging accuracy and fitness (AASL.VI.A.3, g6-lateral-reading, grade 6). Out: subject terms and following references (unit 3); a self-set topic (grade 10); machine-made text (unit 4). Opening retrievals: day 1 g8-test-assumptions and g8-research-product (write what you expect to find; name last year's product and audience); day 2 g8-multiple-views-gather; day 3 g8-news-lateral; day 4 g8-compare-wikis. Days: show-me (hook: a too-broad and a too-narrow question on the topic; the question designed), show-me (database or open web, operators and filters in the search plan), show-me (the lateral routine card on a polished invented page), practice (three invented claims checked laterally in pairs with the card; an operator string fixed; the source log begun), inquiry (round 2 on the student's own question; log entries; the card withdrawn; the teacher writes feedback on each log before day 6), inquiry (the log rechecked laterally as the question shifts; the shift logged), inquiry (the routine without the card on a hard case from the log; a one-page findings note shared with a partner pair; the self-check). Skill card: "stop, investigate the source, better coverage, trace it." End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year check (day 8) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the revised log scored as its I.D.2 product (Course-level checks). Tools: the research notebook, a closed invented source set of six sources with "other tabs" sheets, an invented database results page, the search plan sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: judging sources of the past, wh1-sources-of-the-past in social studies grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 15), and the short research of ela grade 9 unit 4 (days 67 to 84); stand-alone: "How much do street trees cool a town block in summer?" inside the teacher-set topic "trees and heat in towns" | First because every later unit needs a question held over time and sources judged and kept: grade 8 handed on the expectation, the search plan, lateral reading on news and tracing to references, and grade 9 makes them one long research habit; the four lateral days put most of the band's grade 9 lateral time where the WH1 source checks run in weeks 1 to 10 | Candidate: the 9-10 cells on keeping a list of search terms that worked and on predicting the kinds of sources that will answer; the 9-10 bold on using a database's and an article's own features (abstracts, headings) to find the answering part | Cases even across views; the polished fake page is invented, never a real hoax reproduced. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its source set (C12). Open search only on a teacher-guided inquiry day with the stop rule; the teacher page says what the adult does if an unsafe result appears and points to health's steps if a student tells something worrying (APPROACH.md, Section field 1) |
243: | 2 | 9 | Fair to every side. *How do we make a case that is fair to every side and honest about whose work it uses?* | 7 | g9-fair-sides; g9-group-shapes-solution; g9-open-licenses | AASL.II.B.3; AASL.III.A.3; AASL.VI.C.1 | Investigate (perspective), construct, express, reflect | Presenting more than one side fairly, each view at its strongest with its best evidence, before the group's own conclusion (AASL.II.B.3, owned here: "Show more than one side fairly in what you make and present"). A research group writes a short decision memo for a neutral school problem in which the choice follows from what the group found, each step citing a member's evidence, and each student says how the findings changed their first idea (AASL.III.A.3, owned here: "Let what the group found shape how you choose to solve a problem"). Reading the license on a work (all rights reserved, open licenses and their conditions, public domain), sharing or remixing only as it allows, crediting as it asks, and writing a polite permission request when it does not allow a use; fair use in depth, the four questions weighed on harder cases (a remix, a parody, a clip inside a video) where reasonable people differ, never ruled lawful (AASL.VI.C.1, owned here: "Share and remix others' work only as its license allows"). Revisited, owned earlier: comparing sources with different views (AASL.II.B.2, g7-sources-disagree, grade 7); honest, permitted use (AASL.VI.B.1, g8-fair-use-basics, grade 8). Out: choosing a license for one's own work (unit 4); one full citation style for every kind of source (unit 6). Opening retrievals: day 10 g8-multiple-views-gather and the lateral routine (unit 1); day 11 g8-fair-use-basics; day 13 g8-diverse-team. Days: show-me (each view at its strongest on an invented case), show-me (reading a license; the four questions on a harder case; the permission request), practice (six works sorted by what their licenses allow; two fair use cases argued both ways; a one-sided paragraph rewritten fairly), inquiry (groups gather each view's best evidence on the decision; card withdrawn), inquiry (the memo drafted, images and quotes used as their licenses allow, a permission request drafted where needed), inquiry (written feedback from the teacher and one other group; the memo revised), inquiry (the memo presented to its audience, a school group the teacher arranges; each student writes how the findings changed their first idea; the self-check). Skill card: "strongest case for each side, best evidence, then our choice; read the license before you use it." End-of-unit evidence: the year-end check (day 17) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the memo's change line scored as its III.A.3 item (Course-level checks). Tools: invented case cards for each view, a license sort set of six works with their labels, two fair use case cards, the memo frame, the permission request frame (Curio Class writes all). Host: comparing interpretations, wh1-sources-and-interpretations in social studies grade 9 unit 3 (days 31 to 46); the group talk of ela grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 22); stand-alone: "Should our school add water refill stations, and where?" | After unit 1 because fair sides need the judged, logged sources unit 1 taught, and the memo needs a question held over weeks; the license work sits where a product first uses other people's images and quotes. Grade 8 fair use basics lead straight into the depth here | Candidate: the 9-10 bold on retrieving and using images, sound and video by their legal and ethical terms (open-license and public domain image sources, named generically); the 9-10 bold on working with others to research a real problem and propose a change | Cases even across views; the school problem is neutral and no partisan stand is asked. Fair use and licenses are general guidance, never legal advice; students weigh the questions and never rule a use lawful (APPROACH.md item 6, `rights` read). Honest use without shaming: a student who used a work wrongly is helped to fix it privately (item 4). Groups are mixed by each student's first answer, never by background |
244: | 3 | 10 | Who is missing? *Where do I start on my topic, and whose views are missing from what I have found?* | 7 | g10-own-lens; g10-others-views-in; g10-database-deep | AASL.II.A.3; AASL.III.C.2; AASL.II.C.2 | Connect, wonder, investigate (perspective), express, reflect | The grade 10 student sets their own topic and question for the year's sustained project. Before research, a short "where I start" note on how what they have read, where they live and what they care about may shape how they read the topic, and after it whether that happened (AASL.II.A.3, owned here: "Explain how your own background shapes the way you read a topic"). Bringing views not yet heard into the research on purpose: a source or an interview from a missing perspective, added to the source log with a line on why it was missing (AASL.III.C.2, owned here: "Bring other people's views into your own research on purpose"). A seminar where people disagree: turns that add evidence and say back another view first, kept on the record sheet (AASL.II.C.2, owned here: "Add to a talk where people hold different views, and listen as well"). Database subject terms, and references followed both ways (back through a source's list, forward to works that cite it), finding open-access copies. Revisited, owned earlier: weighing a source's point of view (AASL.II.A.2, g6-point-of-view, grade 6); searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4); gathering from sources that see the question differently (AASL.IV.B.2, g8-multiple-views-gather, grade 8). Out: interviewing someone whose life differs as the unit's main source (grade 11); mapping the communities a topic touches (grade 12). Opening retrievals: day 19 g9-research-question-design (unit 1); day 20 g9-databases-operators (unit 1); day 22 g9-fair-sides and g9-group-shapes-solution (unit 2). Days: show-me (the self-set topic and question; the "where I start" note modeled on an invented student, then written privately), show-me (subject terms and reference chains in a database), practice (subject terms and both-way chains on an invented database page; one missing view found in a reference list), show-me (seminar ground rules: say back another view, then add evidence), inquiry (the student's own search for a missing perspective, a source or an interview under the interview rules; log line on why it was missing; card withdrawn), inquiry (the seminar on the host or stand-alone question, turns on the record sheet), inquiry (findings so far shared with a partner; the after-note on the starting point; the self-check). Skill card: "who have I heard, who is missing, where would they speak?" End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year check (day 26) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the seminar record (day 24) and the log's missing-view line scored as its products (Course-level checks). Tools: an invented database results page with subject terms and reference lists, the "where I start" sheet, the seminar record sheet, a stand-alone source set of six views (Curio Class writes all). Host: the seminar of ela grade 10 unit 1 (days 1 to 28), e10-sl-seminar; stand-alone: "What should a town do with an empty lot?", with invented sources from neighbors, shop owners, parents, an older resident with no car and a planner | First in grade 10 because the self-set topic needs its starting point named before the research begins, and missing views are easiest to add while the source log is young; subject terms and references are the search move that finds them. It runs beside ELA's seminar in weeks 1 to 6 | Candidate: the 9-10 cells on examining one's own assumptions about the topic and on noticing that one's own experience shapes the meaning taken from media; the 9-10 bold on confidence in questioning views unlike one's own | No student is asked to disclose identity, religion, family or health details; each chooses what to share, and the "where I start" note may stay private to the teacher, with an invented reader's note as the other path. Interviews follow APPROACH.md item 8 (neutral topic, questions checked by the teacher, consent, a parent's consent under 18, no full name). Ground rules for disagreement are set before the seminar. Cases even across views |
245: | 4 | 10 | Putting sources together. *What do my sources say together, and can I trust a machine-made summary of them?* | 7 | g10-synthesis-map; g10-lateral-machine-text; g10-label-own-work | AASL.IV.D.2; AASL.VI.B.3 | Investigate, construct, express, reflect | A synthesis matrix (sources down, subquestions across) and a map of how the ideas connect: where sources agree, conflict and build on each other, and where the gap is (AASL.IV.D.2, owned here: "Map how ideas from different sources connect into one picture"). Checking machine-made text against its sources without the routine card: each claim of a teacher-supplied machine-made summary (a dated capture or an invented copy; no student uses a tool or needs an account) traced to a source, claims with no source or a wrong one marked. Labeling one's own research product so others can credit it: maker (initials or a class name in public), date, a chosen license, and full credits for what it uses (AASL.VI.B.3, owned here: "Label your own work so others can credit it correctly"). Revisited, owned earlier: staying with one question (AASL.I.D.2, g9-research-question-design, unit 1); judging accuracy and fitness (AASL.VI.A.3, g6-lateral-reading, grade 6); sharing as a license allows (AASL.VI.C.1, g9-open-licenses, unit 2). Out: one full citation style for every kind of source and a method note (grade 11); a disclosure line for one's own tool use (grade 11). Opening retrievals: day 28 g9-source-log (unit 1); day 30 g9-lateral-method (unit 1); day 33 g9-open-licenses (unit 2). Days: show-me (the matrix and the connection map), practice (matrix and map from four short invented sources on light pollution; the gap named), show-me (tracing a machine-made summary's claims to sources), practice (two captured summaries traced, claims marked), inquiry (the student's own project: matrix and map; a dated capture of a summary on the topic traced; card withdrawn), show-me (labeling one's own work: initials or class name, date, a chosen license, credits), inquiry (the finished labeled product shared with another class; the self-check). Skill card: "agree, conflict, build, gap; trace every claim; label what I made." End-of-unit evidence: the year-end check (day 35) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks). Tools: the matrix sheet and map frame, the light pollution source set, three teacher-made machine-made summaries as dated captures with their source pages, a license label card (Curio Class writes all). Host: the research project of ela grade 10 unit 7 (days 128 to 155), e10-w-research; the research argument of World History II unit 11 (days 145 to 157), wh2-research-argument; stand-alone: "How much brighter are our night skies than they were, and does it matter?" | After unit 3 because the matrix needs the year's sources, missing views included, gathered over weeks 2 to 13; the second half of the band's lateral time comes here, without the card, on the newest kind of source; the label goes on the product the matrix produces, last | Candidate: the 9-10 bold on identifying misinformation by the motives of its maker; the 9-10 cell on telling sponsored, opinion and news content apart, on a captured page | No claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable (APPROACH.md item 7); no student uses a tool or needs an account. Privacy first: no full names or photos on public work. Licenses are general guidance, never legal advice (`rights` read) |
246: | 5 | 11 | Beyond the article, and news that moves. *Where is the evidence that articles only report, and how do you judge a story that is still changing?* | 5 | g11-advanced-search; g11-lateral-news | None | Investigate, construct, reflect | Searching beyond articles: government and agency reports, data sets and primary data, saving searches to rerun. Following a fast-moving invented or dated news case over several days of captures, holding judgment, checking updates and corrections laterally, and rechecking the source log. Revisited, owned earlier: searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4); judging accuracy and fitness in context (AASL.VI.A.3, g6-lateral-reading, grade 6); rechecking a collection of sources (AASL.IV.D.1, g9-source-log, unit 1). Out: building saved searches into a personal system (grade 12); a method note (unit 6). Opening retrievals: day 37 g10-database-deep (unit 3); day 39 g10-lateral-machine-text (unit 4). Days: show-me (reports, data sets and primary data; a saved search), practice (a report and a data set found on an invented agency results page; a primary data table contrasted with a news account of it; a search saved and rerun), show-me (holding judgment on breaking news over dated captures), inquiry (the invented bridge-closure case followed through four dated captures; the log rechecked; card withdrawn), inquiry (on the student's own question, one report or data set found and logged and one fast-news claim checked; shared with a partner; the self-check). Skill card: "who first published the numbers; what changed since; hold judgment until it is checked." End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year check (day 42) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks). Tools: an invented agency results page with reports and data sets, a data table and a news account of it, four dated captures of the bridge case (Curio Class writes all). Host: the news reading of US History I unit 9 (days 140 to 155), us1-news-reading; the research inquiry of US History I unit 1 (days 1 to 19); stand-alone: "How much water does farming in one dry region use, and has it changed?" | First in grade 11 because the year's short project (unit 6) needs reports and data in its log and a habit of holding judgment, and both build straight on grade 10's database and machine-text work; it runs while US History I opens its research inquiry in weeks 1 to 4 | Candidate: the 11-12 bold on reading laterally across primary and secondary sources for the context of a time and its people; the 11-12 cell on recognizing when an item has gone viral and how it was made | News cases invented or dated and chosen evenly across views; a school may swap a case. The bridge case has no injuries and no named people. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its source set (C12) |
247: | 6 | 11 | Show how you know. *How can a reader check how you found, chose and used your sources, and was your work made honestly?* | 9 | g11-method-note; g11-learn-across-difference; g11-publish-for-audience; g11-honesty-review | AASL.IV.D.3; AASL.II.D.1; AASL.VI.C.2; AASL.VI.D.2 | Investigate (people as sources), construct, express, reflect | A method note and an annotated bibliography that show how the student found, chose and sorted their sources, so a reader could check the work, in one full citation style for every kind of source used (a report, a data set, an interview, any machine-made text), on a short project (AASL.IV.D.3, owned here: "Show others how you chose and sorted your sources so they can check"). Choosing to learn from people whose experience of the topic differs (a job, a place, an age, a way of living with it): planning and holding an interview with consent, or reading a set of first-person sources, and reporting what changed in one's thinking (AASL.II.D.1, owned here: "Choose to learn with and from people whose lives differ from yours"). Publishing the research in a form that suits a chosen audience (a one-page brief, an infographic, a recorded talk, an exhibit) and explaining the choice (AASL.VI.C.2, owned here: "Publish what you found in a form that suits its audience"). Looking back on the finished project: every quote in its context, credit complete, sources represented fairly, a disclosure line if a machine-made text tool was used (using one is never required) (AASL.VI.D.2, owned here: "Look back on whether your research was made honestly and fairly"). Revisited, owned earlier: sharing with people who have a reason to care (AASL.I.C.4, g5-share-real-audience, grade 5); honest, permitted use (AASL.VI.B.1, g8-fair-use-basics, grade 8). Out: the capstone's method note and publishing (grade 12); a recommendation for a decision (grade 12). Opening retrievals: day 44 g10-synthesis-map (unit 4); day 45 g10-own-lens and g10-others-views-in (unit 3); day 49 g10-label-own-work (unit 4); day 51 g10-lateral-machine-text (unit 4). Days: show-me (the method note and annotated bibliography; one style for four kinds of source), show-me (learning from someone whose experience differs: the interview plan, consent, questions checked by the teacher, or the first-person set), practice (citations for a report, a data set, an interview and a machine-made text; two sources annotated), inquiry (the interview held, through the school or a family, or the first-person set read; notes; card withdrawn), inquiry (the method note and bibliography drafted for the short project; written feedback before the next session), show-me (choosing a published form for an audience), inquiry (the method note revised from feedback; the published form made), inquiry (the honesty review, first on an invented finished project, then on one's own; fixes made privately), inquiry (the work published to its audience; what changed in the student's thinking reported; the self-check). Skill card: "how I found it, why I kept it, how a reader checks it; is every quote in context and every credit there?" End-of-unit evidence: the year-end check (day 53) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the revised method note and bibliography scored as its IV.D.3 product and the interview report as its II.D.1 product (Course-level checks). Tools: the method note frame, a citation guide for one full style, the interview planning sheet and consent note, an invented first-person source set from night-shift workers, an invented finished project with planted honesty faults, publishing format cards (Curio Class writes all). Host: the reform research project of ela grade 11 unit 4 (days 56 to 78), e11-research and e11-presentation; the research inquiry of US History I unit 1 (days 1 to 19), us1-research-inquiry; stand-alone: "What does it take to work a night shift?" | After unit 5 because the method note shows the reports, data and fast-news checks unit 5 taught, and its bibliography needs their citations; learning across difference comes before publishing so the interview shapes what is published; the honesty review comes last, on finished work | Candidate: the 11-12 bold on drafting the product to carry the line of reasoning and its evidence; the 11-12 bold on judging one's own product and process with self, teacher and peer feedback | Interviews follow APPROACH.md item 8 and the row note: the difference is in experience of the topic, never chosen by a group label, and no group is treated as one voice; consent, a parent's consent under 18, a student 18 or over gives their own, no full name; the first-person set is the full no-interview path. Published work uses initials or a class name. Honesty problems are fixed privately, never named before the class (item 4). No claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable (item 7) |
248: | 7 | 12 | A system for after school. *How will you find and check information when no teacher sets the question, and help someone else do it?* | 6 | g12-personal-system; g12-help-others | AASL.VI.D.1; AASL.VI.D.3 | Connect, investigate, express, reflect | Setting up one's own way to find and use information after school: saved searches, a short list of sources checked laterally, a note system and a habit of checking a claim before sharing it, run on a new question (AASL.VI.D.1, owned here: "Set up your own ways of finding and using information that work"). Helping others use information safely, honestly and within the law: teaching a younger class or a family member one lateral check, the stop rule or how to credit, with a short guide the student made (AASL.VI.D.3, owned here: "Help others use information safely, honestly and within the law"). Out: the capstone decision (unit 8). Opening retrievals: day 55 g11-advanced-search (unit 5); day 57 g11-honesty-review (unit 6). Days: show-me (a personal system: saved searches, a checked list, notes, the check-before-share habit), practice (the system built and run on a teacher question, plus one search the student chooses on a next step after school, a training, a program or a job, kept private), show-me (teaching one move to someone younger: the short guide), inquiry (the guide drafted; written feedback before the next session; card withdrawn), inquiry (the guide revised; the teaching rehearsed with a partner), inquiry (the move taught to a younger class the teacher arranges, or to a family member at home; shared; the self-check). Skill card: "save it, check it, note it, pause before you share." End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year check (day 61) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the revised guide scored as its VI.D.3 product (Course-level checks). Tools: the personal system sheet, a guide frame, an invented question set for the system run (Curio Class writes all). Host: the reading and research routines of ela grade 12 unit 1 (days 1 to 15); stand-alone: "Which training or program after school fits a goal you choose, and how do you check what its pages promise?" | First in grade 12 because the personal system gathers the grade 11 searches and checks while they are fresh, and the guide is taught to younger students in the fall while visits are easiest to arrange; the capstone then runs on the student's own system | Candidate: the 11-12 bold on choosing materials by one's own interests and plans; the 11-12 bold on taking a leadership role that helps others do their best work | The next-step search is the student's own choice and stays private to the student; no student is asked about family money or plans. Visits to younger classes are teacher-arranged; copyright points stay general guidance, never legal advice. The stop rule is taught to younger children in its K to 2 words (close, tell, no blame) |
249: | 8 | 12 | The capstone. *What should be decided, who does the decision touch, and what does the evidence say?* | 8 | g12-capstone-decision; g12-community-stand; g12-lateral-capstone | AASL.I.D.3; AASL.II.D.3 | Connect, wonder, investigate, construct, express, reflect | The capstone: a long inquiry put to use on a real school or community decision or a next step after school, a recommendation tied to evidence, with the method note and annotated bibliography, published in the form grade 11 practiced for the people the decision affects (AASL.I.D.3, owned here: "Put what research taught you to use on a real problem or decision"). Mapping the communities the topic touches (users, workers, neighbors, future users) and saying where the student stands among them and what follows for the conclusion (AASL.II.D.3, owned here: "Think about where you stand among the many communities a topic touches"). Auditing a classmate's capstone source list: each source checked laterally, two claims traced upstream, the citations checked; then fixing one's own from the audit received. Revisited, owned earlier: staying with one question (AASL.I.D.2, g9-research-question-design, unit 1); publishing for an audience (AASL.VI.C.2, g11-publish-for-audience, unit 6); judging accuracy and fitness (AASL.VI.A.3, g6-lateral-reading, grade 6); showing how sources were chosen (AASL.IV.D.3, g11-method-note, unit 6). Out: nothing later in the subject; the thread ends here (Seams). Opening retrievals: day 63 g11-method-note, g11-publish-for-audience and g11-learn-across-difference (unit 6); day 66 g11-lateral-news (unit 5). Days: show-me (the capstone decision; the communities map; where I stand), inquiry (search rounds and log on the capstone question, on the student's own system; card withdrawn), inquiry (the recommendation drafted with its method note and bibliography), practice (the lateral audit of a classmate's source list, written as feedback), inquiry (one's own capstone fixed from the audit and the teacher's written feedback), inquiry (the published form made for the people affected), inquiry (the capstone published and shared with its audience; the self-check), end-of-unit assessment (day 70: the capstone scored on its rubric, with a short sitting on the student's own capstone: trace one claim upstream, an unseen source audited, where they stand and what follows). Skill card: "decision, evidence, who it touches, where I stand, checked by a peer." End-of-unit evidence: day 70, inside the unit, so the score stands if seniors leave early (Grade 12 calendar). Tools: the capstone planning sheet, the communities map, the audit sheet, the capstone rubric (Curio Class writes all). Host: the senior research project of ela grade 12 unit 8 (days 125 to 160), e12-research; the researched argument of US History II unit 12 (days 148 to 160), us2-research-paper; stand-alone: "How should our school spend a one-time grant for student spaces?" | Last because it puts the whole band to use: the question held over weeks (unit 1), fair sides (unit 2), missing views and synthesis (units 3 and 4), reports, method note and publishing (units 5 and 6) and the personal system (unit 7). It ends by week 29 so the scored capstone stands before seniors leave | Candidate: the 11-12 bold on a line of argument that takes in or answers competing interpretations with credible evidence; the 11-12 bold on corroborating across diverse sources and balancing perspectives | The problem is chosen evenly across views and no partisan stand is required. Mapping communities asks no student to disclose identity, religion or family details; where they stand is said about the topic, never about who they are. A student 18 or over gives their own consent for any interview; published work uses initials or a class name. The audit is taught without shaming: a weak source is fixed, never named before the class |
