exp,K,k-wonder,AASL.V.C.1,,g1-what-i-know,,,wonder; question,social-studies:ssk-asking-questions (the same habit of asking about the world),"Children look closely at a picture, an object or a short read-aloud on a neutral topic (seeds, snails, rain) and say one ""I wonder..."" question of their own; the teacher writes each on a class wonder board that later units draw from. Checked alone in kindergarten: each child says a question, not a statement, about the day's topic, recorded on the teacher's checklist. Later grades carry it as they turn wonders into research questions."
cur,8,g8-multiple-views-gather,AASL.IV.B.2; ~AASL.IV.A.3,g7-sources-disagree; 
g7-search-results,g9-databases-operators; g9-fair-sides,search-plan,search-plan,perspective; stakeholder; other 
side,ela:e8-conflicting-viewpoints (how an author responds to conflicting viewpoints),"Students plan searches on 
purpose to gather sources that see the question differently (an expert body, people affected, a critic) and record in 
the search plan which view each source brings. Checked alone in grade 8. Owned here. Teacher note: questions are 
invented or chosen evenly across views."
cur,8,g8-news-lateral,~AASL.VI.A.3,g7-lateral-hard-cases,g9-lateral-method,lateral-reading,lateral-reading,news; 
report; opinion; fact-checking,social-studies:ss8-news-literacy (news and media literacy),"Students apply lateral 
reading to news items alongside the social studies news unit: tell a report from an opinion piece, check the outlet 
laterally, and trace a claim upstream. Library taught the method in grades 5 to 7; social studies uses it on news the 
same year. Either order; the second course opens with a retrieval task on the first. Teacher note: news cases are 
invented or chosen evenly across views; a school may swap a case. Cases include a news report, an opinion piece, a 
blog post and a viral video (described or an invented still), each checked by the same method (the continuum's grade 8 
cell on methods by kind of source)."
cur,8,g8-compare-wikis,AASL.IV.C.3,g7-class-wiki,g9-source-log,,,compare; group-written; trace; reference,,"With 
classmates, students compare what several group-written sites say on one question, list the differences, and trace 
each difference back to the references behind it. Checked alone in grade 8. Owned here."
eng,8,g8-fair-use-basics,AASL.VI.B.1; ~AASL.VI.A.2,g7-citation-style,g9-open-licenses,citation,citation,copyright; 
fair use; permission; public domain,ela:e8-self-generated-research (citing sources in a research project),"Students 
learn the basics of copyright and fair use: a work is protected from the moment it is made; fair use weighs four 
questions (why the work is used, what kind of work it is, how much is used, and whether the use takes the place of the 
original), and giving credit alone does not make a use fair. For invented cases (an image, a clip, a quote in their 
product) students say which way each question leans and pick the safer path: a public domain or openly licensed work, 
a smaller part, a link to the work instead of a copy, or asking permission. Checked alone in grade 8: the four 
questions applied to a new case and a safer path chosen. Owned here. Teacher note: general guidance, never legal 
advice; students weigh the questions and never rule a use permitted or lawful (APPROACH Sensitive ground 6)."
exp,8,g8-test-assumptions,AASL.V.A.2; 
~AASL.I.D.1,g7-evidence-discussion,g9-research-question-design,research-cycle,research-cycle,assumption; expect; 
surprise; change my mind,ela:e8-self-generated-research (research from self-generated questions); 
science:s8-human-trait-technology (host task: gathering reliable information on a technology),"Before searching, 
students write what they expect to find; during research they mark each finding that tests that belief and say what 
they now think and why. Checked alone in grade 8: an expectation, the evidence against it, and an honest update. Owned 
here. Students also say how their own first view shaped what they looked for and trusted (the continuum's grade 8 cell 
on one's own point of view; the fuller where-I-start note is grade 10, g10-own-lens)."
col,8,g8-diverse-team,AASL.II.B.1; AASL.III.B.2,g7-group-solutions,g9-group-shapes-solution,,,teammate; different 
view; exchange,ela:e8-collab-discussion (discussion with diverse partners),"Students work in mixed teams on a question 
where they see things differently, and link with another class (through a teacher-run exchange) to trade findings and 
build on what each class already knows. Teams are mixed by each student's first answer to the question, written before 
research, never by background. Where no partner class exists, the exchange is with another section, another grade, or 
a findings set left by an earlier class. Checked alone in grade 8: each student shows one change a teammate's 
different view made (II.B.1) and one change the other class's findings made (III.B.2). Owned here. Teacher note: 
exchanges run through the teachers; no student shares contact details."
inq,8,g8-research-product,~AASL.I.B.3; ~AASL.I.C.4; 
~AASL.III.C.1,g7-feedback-loop,g9-research-question-design,research-cycle,research-cycle,research product; audience; 
comments,ela:e8-present-reasoning (presenting findings with sound reasoning),"The 6 to 8 course ends with a full 
research product shared with a real audience (a younger class, families, a school group), with comments asked for and 
answered in a response log. Grade 8 runs the whole cycle with the least help in the band. Before the search plan, 
students sort their research questions by the kind of information each asks for (overview, specific detail, cause and 
effect, comparison), the continuum's grade 8 question cell."
inq,9,g9-research-question-design,AASL.I.D.2; ~AASL.I.A.1; ~AASL.I.B.2,g8-test-assumptions; 
g8-research-product,g10-synthesis-map,research-cycle,research-cycle,research question; scope; round; 
log,ela:e9-w-short-research (short and sustained research projects),"Students design a research question narrow enough 
to answer and open enough to matter, and stay with it over several weeks and several rounds of searching, logging how 
the question shifts. Checked alone in grade 9. Owned here. Grade 9 works inside a topic the teacher sets; grade 10 
sets its own topic."
cur,9,g9-databases-operators,~AASL.IV.B.1; 
~AASL.IV.A.3,g8-multiple-views-gather,g10-database-deep,search-plan,search-plan,database; operator; AND; OR; NOT; 
filter,,"Students search a database (a free public library or open-access one, named generically, or an invented one 
on the print path) with operators (AND, OR, NOT, exact phrase) and filters by date and kind, and compare it with the 
open web for the same question. Grade 9 learns the operators and filters; grade 10 adds subject terms and following 
references. Teacher note: no paid database or library access is assumed. Truncation is one of the operators."
cur,9,g9-lateral-method,~AASL.VI.A.3; 
~AASL.IV.B.3,g8-news-lateral,g10-lateral-machine-text,lateral-reading,lateral-reading,stop; investigate; trace; better 
coverage,social-studies:wh1-sources-of-the-past (host task: judging sources of the past),"Lateral reading on hard 
cases as a fast routine: stop, investigate the source, look for better coverage, trace the claim to its original. 
Grades 9 and 10 together hold at least 300 minutes of lateral reading (seven 45-minute sessions), the amount in the 
one high school field study (six 50-minute lessons). Grade 9 runs the routine with the card on invented cases; grade 
10 without it, on machine-made content. Teacher note: cases chosen evenly across views; hoax pages described, never 
reproduced. The continuum bolds lateral reading for context at 11-12; library teaches the method from grade 5, and 
grades 11 and 12 return to it."
cur,9,g9-source-log,AASL.IV.D.1,g8-compare-wikis,g10-synthesis-map,lateral-reading,,annotated log; recheck; keep; 
drop,,"Students keep an annotated source log for a long research question, with a judgment line and the date each 
source was last checked, and recheck it as the question shifts, dropping or adding sources with a reason. Checked 
alone in grade 9. Owned here."
eng,9,g9-open-licenses,AASL.VI.C.1; ~AASL.VI.B.1,g8-fair-use-basics,g10-label-own-work,citation,citation,copyright; 
fair use; open license; public domain; remix; permission,,"Students read the license on a work (all rights reserved, 
open licenses with their conditions, public domain), share or remix only as it allows, credit as the license asks, and 
write a polite request for permission when a license does not allow a use. Fair use in depth: the four questions 
weighed on harder cases (a remix, a parody, a clip inside a video), where reasonable people differ. Checked alone in 
grade 9. Owned here. Teacher note: general guidance, never legal advice; no claim that a use is lawful. Checked at 
9-10 depth (copyright, fair use, permission); the 11-12 bold on open licenses returns in g10-label-own-work and 
g11-honesty-review."
inc,9,g9-fair-sides,AASL.II.B.3; ~AASL.II.B.2,g8-multiple-views-gather,g10-own-lens; g10-others-views-in,,,fair; side; 
represent; strongest case,social-studies:wh1-sources-and-interpretations (comparing interpretations),"In the research 
product students present more than one side fairly, stating each view at its strongest with its best evidence before 
giving their own conclusion. Checked alone in grade 9. Owned here. Teacher note: cases are invented or chosen evenly 
across views."
col,9,g9-group-shapes-solution,AASL.III.A.3,g8-diverse-team,g10-others-views-in,,,decision; group findings; 
recommend,ela:e9-sl-discussion (collaborative discussion),"A research group writes a short decision memo for a neutral 
school or community problem in which the choice follows from what the group found, each step citing the member's 
evidence. Checked alone in grade 9: each student explains how the group's findings changed their own first idea. Owned 
here."
cur,10,g10-synthesis-map,AASL.IV.D.2; ~AASL.I.D.2,g9-source-log; g9-research-question-design,g11-method-note; 
g11-publish-for-audience,research-cycle,research-cycle,synthesis; matrix; connect; gap,ela:e10-w-research 
(synthesizing multiple sources),"Students build a synthesis matrix (sources down, subquestions across) and a map of 
how the ideas connect: where sources agree, conflict and build on each other, and where the gap is. Grade 10 sets its 
own topic and question for the sustained project (grade 9 worked inside a topic the teacher set). Checked alone in 
grade 10. Owned here."
inc,10,g10-own-lens,AASL.II.A.3; ~AASL.II.A.2,g9-fair-sides,g11-learn-across-difference,,,background; lens; starting 
point,,"Before a research unit students write a short ""where I start"" note on how what they have read, where they 
live and what they care about may shape how they read the topic, and after it say whether that happened. Checked alone 
in grade 10. Owned here. Teacher note: no student is asked to disclose identity, religion, family or health details; 
each chooses what to share, and the note may stay private to the teacher."
inc,10,g10-others-views-in,AASL.III.C.2; AASL.II.C.2,g9-fair-sides; 
g9-group-shapes-solution,g11-learn-across-difference,,,perspective; seminar; listen; build,ela:e10-sl-seminar (seminar 
discussion with diverse views),"Students bring views not yet heard into their research on purpose (a source or 
interview from a missing perspective) and take part in a seminar where people disagree, adding evidence and listening 
back. Checked alone in grade 10: a source from a missing perspective added to the source log with a line on why it was 
missing (III.C.2), and seminar turns on the record sheet that add evidence and say back another view first (II.C.2). 
Owns both. Teacher note: interview rules, APPROACH Sensitive ground 8; cases chosen evenly across views; ground rules 
for disagreement are set first."
cur,10,g10-database-deep,~AASL.IV.B.1; 
~AASL.IV.B.2,g9-databases-operators,g11-advanced-search,search-plan,search-plan,subject term; reference list; cited 
by; open access,,"Students use subject terms in a database and follow references both ways (back through a source's 
reference list, forward to works that cite it), finding open-access copies. Grade 10 adds subject terms and following 
references to grade 9's operators."
cur,10,g10-lateral-machine-text,~AASL.VI.A.3,g9-lateral-method,g11-honesty-review; 
g11-lateral-news,lateral-reading,lateral-reading,machine-made text; summary; check; 
disclose,social-studies:wh2-research-argument (host task: a research argument),"Students check machine-made text 
against its sources: they trace each claim of a machine-made summary to a source and mark claims with no source or a 
wrong one, without the routine card. The summary is supplied by the teacher as a dated capture or an invented copy; no 
student uses a tool or needs an account. Grade 10 is the second half of the band's lateral reading time. Teacher note: 
no claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable."
eng,10,g10-label-own-work,AASL.VI.B.3; ~AASL.VI.C.1,g9-open-licenses,g11-honesty-review,citation,citation,label; 
license; credit; date,,"Students label their own research product so others can credit it: maker (initials or class 
name in public), date, a chosen license, and full credits for what it uses. Checked alone in grade 10. Owned here. 
Teacher note: privacy first, no full names or photos on public work. Credits follow the grade 7 citation style 
(g7-citation-style), embedded at the point of use."
cur,11,g11-method-note,AASL.IV.D.3,g10-synthesis-map,g12-capstone-decision,search-plan; citation,citation,method; 
annotated bibliography; transparent; citation style,social-studies:us1-research-inquiry (host task: a research 
inquiry),"Students write a method note and annotated bibliography that show how they found, chose and sorted their 
sources, so a reader could check the work. The annotated bibliography uses one full citation style for every kind of 
source used, including a report, a data set, an interview and any machine-made text. Checked alone in grade 11 on a 
short project; grade 12 writes it for the capstone. Owned here."
eng,11,g11-honesty-review,AASL.VI.D.2; ~AASL.VI.B.1,g10-label-own-work; 
g10-lateral-machine-text,g12-help-others,,,disclose; integrity; context; fair,ela:e11-research (gathering and 
integrating sources while avoiding plagiarism),"Students look back on a finished project to judge whether it was made 
honestly and fairly: every quote in its context, credit complete, sources represented fairly, and a disclosure line if 
a machine-made text tool was used (using one is never required). Checked alone in grade 11. Owned here. Teacher note: 
taught without shaming; problems are fixed privately; no claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable."
inq,11,g11-publish-for-audience,AASL.VI.C.2; 
~AASL.I.C.4,g10-synthesis-map,g12-capstone-decision,research-cycle,research-cycle,publish; format; audience; 
brief,ela:e11-presentation (presenting information for a purpose and audience),"Students publish their research in a 
form that suits a chosen audience (a one-page brief, an infographic, a recorded talk, an exhibit) and explain the 
choice. Checked alone in grade 11. Owned here; grade 12 publishes the capstone. Teacher note: published work uses 
initials or a class name, never a student's full name or photo."
inc,11,g11-learn-across-difference,AASL.II.D.1,g10-own-lens; g10-others-views-in,g12-community-stand,,,perspective; 
interview; consent; listen,social-studies:us1-research-inquiry (host task: a research inquiry),"Students choose to 
learn from people whose lives differ from theirs: they plan and hold an interview or read a set of first-person 
sources from people with different experience of the topic, with consent, and report what changed in their thinking. 
Checked alone in grade 11. Owned here. Teacher note: interview rules, APPROACH Sensitive ground 8; the difference is 
in experience of the topic (a job, a place, an age, a way of living with it), never chosen by a group label; no group 
is treated as one voice."
cur,11,g11-lateral-news,~AASL.VI.A.3; 
~AASL.IV.D.1,g10-lateral-machine-text,g12-lateral-capstone,lateral-reading,lateral-reading,breaking news; update; hold 
judgment,social-studies:us1-news-reading (news reading),"Students follow a fast-moving invented or dated news case 
over several days, holding judgment, checking updates and corrections laterally, and rechecking their source log. 
Grade 11 is the time-pressure case; grade 12 applies the checks to the capstone. Teacher note: cases chosen evenly 
across views; a school may swap a case. Judge the degree of slant, from a mild lean to propaganda."
cur,11,g11-advanced-search,~AASL.IV.B.1,g10-database-deep,g12-personal-system,search-plan,search-plan,report; data 
set; primary data; alert,,"Students search beyond articles: government and agency reports, data sets and primary data, 
saving searches to rerun. Grade 11 finds them; grade 12 builds them into a personal system. Contrast a primary data 
source with a secondary account of it; revisit how results pages order and mark sponsored results (g5-search-revise)."
inq,12,g12-capstone-decision,AASL.I.D.3; ~AASL.I.D.2; ~AASL.VI.C.2,g11-method-note; 
g11-publish-for-audience,,research-cycle,,capstone; recommendation; evidence; 
decision,social-studies:us2-research-paper (host task: a research paper); ela:e12-research (sustained research 
projects),"The capstone: students put a long inquiry to use on a real problem or decision (a school or community 
choice, a next step after school) and write a recommendation tied to evidence, with the method note and annotated 
bibliography. The capstone is published for the people the decision affects, in the form grade 11 practiced. Checked 
alone in grade 12. Owned here. Teacher note: the problem is chosen evenly across views and no partisan stand is 
required."
eng,12,g12-personal-system,AASL.VI.D.1,g11-advanced-search,,search-plan; lateral-reading,,system; saved search; 
trusted list; routine,,"Students set up their own way to find and use information after school: saved searches, a 
short list of sources they checked laterally, a note system and a habit for checking a claim before sharing it. 
Checked alone in grade 12: the system run on a new question. Owned here. One next-step search for after school and one 
goal set from the student's own strengths, both kept private."
eng,12,g12-help-others,AASL.VI.D.3,g11-honesty-review,,,,teach; guide; safely; honestly,,"Students help others use 
information safely, honestly and within the law: they teach a younger class or a family member one lateral check, the 
stop rule or how to credit, with a short guide they made. Checked alone in grade 12. Owned here. Teacher note: visits 
to younger classes are teacher-arranged; copyright points stay general guidance, never legal advice."
inc,12,g12-community-stand,AASL.II.D.3,g11-learn-across-difference,,,,community; affected; position,,"Students map the 
communities a research topic touches (users, workers, neighbors, future users) and say where they stand among them and 
what follows for their conclusion. Checked alone in grade 12. Owned here. Teacher note: no student is asked to 
disclose identity, religion or family details; topics are chosen evenly across views."
cur,12,g12-lateral-capstone,~AASL.VI.A.3; ~AASL.IV.D.3,g11-lateral-news,,lateral-reading; citation,,audit; source 
check; peer,,"Students audit a classmate's capstone source list: check each source laterally, trace two claims 
upstream and check the citations, then fix their own from the audit they receive. Grade 12 applies the band's checks 
to finished work."
