docs/APPROACH.md:44:## The role of the standards
docs/APPROACH.md:55:### Which standards: national, not federal (from the Phase 1 scope, 2026-10-22)
docs/APPROACH.md:57:There are no federal K-12 content standards. Federal law (ESSA, and earlier the Department of Education Organization Act) bars the federal government from setting curriculum, and each state adopts its own standards. The highest level that exists is the **national consensus framework** for each subject, written by states, teachers or professional associations. Most state standards are adopted from or modeled on these. That is our alignment target everywhere, which keeps us from tying the site to any one state.
docs/APPROACH.md:59:Where a subject has no national framework (Precalculus, Statistics, Calculus), the top level is the College Board AP course framework, which every state recognizes.
docs/APPROACH.md:72:| CTE | Advance CTE Modernized Career Clusters (2024, 14 clusters) | framework | `CTE.Career Clusters` | 2024 framework (replaced 16 clusters) |
docs/APPROACH.md:75:| SEL | CASEL framework | competency | `CASEL.Self-Management` | 5 competencies |
docs/APPROACH.md:78:| Precalc / Stats / Calc | College Board AP course frameworks | unit | `AP-STAT.U5` | Unit numbers |
