1: # Cory's decision baseline
2: 
3: Cory's standing rules for deciding questions in Curio Class, drawn from his answers on the decision board (raw
4: answers: `answers.json`, id -> choice and note; questions: `decisions.json`) and from his later decisions in
5: DESK-ANSWERS.md. Rule numbers are cited across the repository and never change.
6: 
7: Every spec, build and review agent reads this before deciding anything itself. Settle questions with these rules.
8: Bring Cory only questions these rules cannot settle and that matter (see "What goes to Cory").
9: 
10: ## The rules
11: 
12: 1. **Quality over saved work.** Never merge, shorten or share something only to save effort. Spans, shared engines and
13:    shared figures are fine only when nothing is lost for any grade or subject. If something is lost, build it
14:    separately (gf-04, w1-21).
15: 2. **Every standard is taught well, above the minimum.** A code on a topic must be really taught in that tool. If it
16:    cannot be taught well there, remove it from the topic and make sure another tool teaches it well. Never add a thin
17:    activity just to make a code honest (gf-07, w2-01, w2-21, w2-22, w1-15).
18: 3. **Better graphics by default.** When there is a choice between drawn-in-code placeholders and real illustrated art,
19:    choose the better art (w1-42).
20: 4. **Every tool stands alone.** The site is public and a student, parent or young child may open any tool with no
21:    teacher present. Defaults must work for a solo user; teacher toggles are extras, never the main experience.
22:    Example: in fair-use-review, the student makes their own call first and Theo's call shows after (w1-53, w1-30, w1-31).
23: 5. **Facts stay plain, with the human side.** Don't soften history or science. Present it with care for the people
24:    involved. Sensitivity and accuracy checks on cultural, Native or hard-history content, in a tool or a course, are
25:    done by a fresh sensitivity agent; once that read passes, the content can go live (proj-03). No outside reader is
26:    looked for. The sensitivity agent reads every unit after studying the community's own published voice and the
27:    scholarship (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md), and we teach with honesty and dignity at an age-appropriate
28:    level (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS).
29: 6. **Classroom-safe language.** No slurs or expletives. A grawlix (#@$!) stands in where a curse would be (w1-28).
30: 7. **Privacy is stated.** Wherever a tool records or stores anything, say on screen that it never leaves the device
31:    (w1-20).
32: 8. **Teacher notes name where the standards cover sensitive topics** (for example, dating in the 6-8 health standards)
33:    (w1-46).
34: 9. **Lean toward the real-world factor.** Show the realistic version by default (the carbon price slider is on by
35:    default in grid-operator) (w3-33).
36: 10. **Good ideas become tools.** When a question surfaces a new tool idea, add it to the concept pipeline for a later
37:     spec wave. Queued: "fact-check a chatbot" (generative AI, from ai-evaluator); a resume builder, near term (w1-02,
38:     w2-12).
39: 11. **People art is drawn, and checked.** Codex may draw people. Every people asset carries `"people": true`, every
40:     people image is on a contact sheet Cory sees before the tool ships, and a fresh agent checks each set for
41:     stereotypes and representation. Reviews add people where a scene needs them.
42: 12. **2 MB is a goal, not a blocker.** Over 6 MB, notes.md says why in a `Size:` line (`scripts/check_tools.py`
43:     enforces only that).
44: 13. **Shared code is grown, not planned.** A kit engine is promoted only after three finished tools use the same code.
45:     Older tools pick it up later and only when needed: they work today, and the gain is efficiency, not capability.
46: 14. **Finish before scaling.** At most about 10 Codex tools built and not yet reviewed. Finish a small cohort end to
47:     end before a new wave, and log repeat problems in the issue ledger.
48: 15. **Public copy names no companies.** The ambition in docs/VISION.md (replace paid curriculum) stays inside the
49:     vision; the site says "a free, complete classroom toolkit".
50: 16. **The first tap may cut the welcome.** A tool does not have to let its welcome line finish after the student's
51:     first tap; acting at once beats listening. Specs, builds and reviews do not flag a welcome cut short by a tap.
52:     What the welcome told the student must still be reachable (the on-screen hint or Read), and a line the tool needs
53:     later (a premise, a role) is said again where it is needed, not only in the welcome.
54: 17. **Curiosities finish on Cory's desk review.** Span and Spire, Pocket Universe and later Curiosities get Cory's
55:     final review on the Review Desk; plate wording (the track was renamed from Instruments to Curiosities on 2026-11-05) and any areas to lift are noted
56:     there and fixed after it, not before. Expect larger design work back on the first two.
57: 18. **Curiosities ship together.** The home page Curiosities entrance is built after every planned Curiosity is fully
58:     built. Each Curiosity's voice goes on the Forge as it passes its exit check, added to the end of the running Forge
59:     queue; they are not held to be recorded together.
60: 19. **Curiosities may keep a little game essence.** Don't be too strict about removing gamification. A Curiosity must
61:     stay purely fun and exploratory; a light game touch (a challenge to try, a surprise, a setup to break) is fine
62:     when it adds to that. A goal ladder, a grade, a forced finish or a game-over still does not belong. Lenses flag a
63:     game element only when it narrows play or turns exploring into scoring.
64: 20. **State adoption is the bar we build to.** Full-course materials (units, Tracks, the Binder and the shelves that
65:     feed them) are built to meet the substance of state and national curriculum reviews. We do not pursue formal
66:     adoption now; if later data says it is a good path, we pivot and are already well placed. The bar never overrides
67:     "What we will not do" in docs/VISION.md (no accounts, tracking or student analytics). What the bar asks, what we
68:     took and what we left out: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md.
69: 21. **A tool teaches a standard only when it passes the teaching test.** A student alone, following the tool's lead,
70:     meets a model, does the standard's own verb (solve, write, explain, record) on every part of its wording, on five
71:     or more items that differ, with a check where thinking is cheaper than guessing, and none of it can be skipped.
72:     For writing and talk a tool cannot mark, a model, a guide while working and a self-check on screen against a shown
73:     guide count. Every tool states a role: `teach` (passes the test), `see why` (lets a student explore the idea and
74:     names what the unit adds) or `practice`. A tool claims a code only for work on its main path; a readout, an
75:     optional mode or a teacher note never carries a code. Recess games and Curiosities take the role `recess` or
76:     `curiosity` and claim no code.
77: 22. **Topics name standards.** A topic page covers one teachable topic. It is split only when it holds two unrelated
78:     topics, and then properly: a split script, redirects for the old pages, and its Word Wall, chart and games divided
79:     by hand (its id keys all of them and about 762 live pages; docs/ECOSYSTEM.md). It is never split just to reach
80:     standard-level detail; the ledger does that. Split candidates come to Cory as exceptions. A topic's standards are
81:     spelled out in place, never a bare cluster, each tool carries its own codes, and data/coverage.csv records where
82:     every standard is taught in full, so an untaught standard shows. The section plan decides how many tools a unit
83:     gets (rule 23); a major unit may run on print with no key tool (grade 4 math unit 3 does). Fluency volume, long
84:     writing, discussion, year-long reading and topic threads across texts belong to units and sections, worksheets
85:     and the Reading Room, and are recorded there, not claimed by a tool.
86: 23. **A section plan comes before a tool.** A tool is the end piece of instruction. No tool is built or refined until
87:     a section plan names its job, and the plan marks it key (the one a day is planned around; built first) or light
88:     (lighter, added over time, same standard of correctness). An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what
89:     the unit needs (rule 1). A teacher judges open work; a tool gives it structure and marks only what has a right
90:     answer. Grades 3 to 12 assume one device per student with a one-line projector path; kindergarten to grade 2 may
91:     assume one shared classroom device, always with a paper path; and a parent teaching one child at home is a named
92:     audience. Always cover the whole standard: a plan teaches and checks every part of each standard it claims, in
93:     both directions where the standard has two (4.NF.A.1: multiplying and dividing both numbers), even where a scope
94:     and sequence title names only one part (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md, rule 19). All of this, with the promises it
95:     rests on, is in docs/FOUNDATION.md, which every spec, unit, section and review agent reads first.
96: 24. **Core subjects first (a soft goal).** Math, English language arts, science and social studies (120 courses: 118
97:     active, 2 world languages shelved; 52 core courses in the four core subjects plus 6 core electives) are the first
98:     focus; the other subjects follow. This bends where work is complementary (a shared rule, a bar page, a standards
99:     fix that serves both). When a choice has to be made, the core subjects come first. Every course is still finished
100:     to the bar; this sets the order, not the scope.
101: 25. **Plan top down, test with a probe and a guard.** The core subjects are planned layer by layer before more days
102:     are written: progressions and course frames for all four together, then scope and sequence and units one subject
103:     at a time (social studies first, math last), each with a review and a baseline. A probe (a few days written from
104:     each subject's units) and a guard (a few days per subject before full writing) test the plans, with "on" and "off"
105:     set beforehand. Sections, days and tools come after. The order is docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, section 3 (settled
106:     2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS, "the build order for the core subjects").
107: 26. **Old tools and specs are sorted by demand.** Three bins, set by script, never by inspection: (a) named by a
108:     section plan, as key or light: gets course work; (b) live and passing the teaching test but not named: stays on
109:     the shelf as a free-standing tool and claims no course coverage; (c) everything else: frozen, no further spend.
110:     Spec fixing in batches has stopped. A tool moves to bin (a) only when a section plan asks for it.
111: 27. **A machine checks what a machine can.** Every keyed item (check items, exit questions, answers, named wrong
112:     answers) is kept as data, and a script recomputes every answer that can be computed. A key the script cannot
113:     reproduce fails the build. "Worked twice by an agent" is not a check. A lesson day is written to one template
114:     (docs/courses/LESSON-DAY.md) so its parts can be counted and checked.
115: 28. **The reads a course needs are named.** docs/FOUNDATION.md section 7 holds the table. Every row is an agent read
116:     (expert, Codex, answer key, safety, sensitivity, rights, Spanish); no outside human reader is planned (settled
117:     2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS). No money is spent and no outside person is approached without Cory's yes.
118: 29. **Course texts are newer and relatable below grade 9.** Kindergarten to grade 8 anchor on newer texts, mostly
119:     original Curio Class texts written to a per-grade text spec and read by the expert agent (not a teacher), plus
120:     recent public-domain government nonfiction; a deeper search for fitting open texts is allowed. Older Reading Room
121:     texts appear there only as a paired or optional read. Grades 9 to 12 may anchor on Reading Room texts, and favor
122:     recognizable and recent literature: beyond the texts the standards name, anchors come from the newest
123:     public-domain works (published 1930 or earlier now, one more year each January), recent open texts, and short
124:     excerpts of well-known recent books; never a library or paid copy. The Reading Room itself stays an
125:     independent-reading shelf (settled 2026-11-12 and 2026-11-13, cards `q-course-texts` and `q-ela-progression-1`,
126:     see DESK-ANSWERS).
127: 30. **A timer needs a grounded purpose.** A timer is allowed where it has a grounded purpose, such as a pace cue, a
128:     movement game or classroom management (the reading pie timers, the movement timer, the projector timer and Game
129:     Night all qualify). The rule is against timers with no purpose (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS).
